article_page('grandsons-property-rights-grandfather', <<<'HTML'
<p>A common question in family property disputes is whether a grandson can claim a share in his grandfather's property simply because he was born into the family. The answer depends on <strong>what kind of property it is</strong> and <strong>how it was inherited</strong>.</p>
<h2>Class I heirs under the Hindu Succession Act</h2>
<p>The Hindu Succession Act, 1956 divides heirs into classes. When a Hindu man dies without a will, his property first goes to his <strong>Class I heirs</strong>: his widow, sons, daughters and mother, among others.</p>
<p>A grandson is a Class I heir only if his own parent (the deceased's son or daughter) has already died. If his father is alive, the father inherits, and the grandson does not take a share directly.</p>
<h2>Self-acquired property: no right by birth</h2>
<p>If the grandfather bought or earned the property himself, it is his <strong>self-acquired property</strong>. He can sell it, gift it or leave it by will to anyone. A grandson has <strong>no right by birth</strong> in it.</p>
<p>When the grandfather dies without a will, the property passes to his Class I heirs under Section 8 of the Act. Courts have held that property a son inherits from his father in this way is generally held by the son as his own, not as ancestral property in which his children get a right by birth.</p>
<h2>Ancestral (coparcenary) property</h2>
<p>The position is different for true ancestral property, meaning property held by an undivided Hindu family that has come down through generations. In such coparcenary property, members of the coparcenary acquire an interest by birth. Since the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005, daughters are also coparceners with the same rights as sons.</p>
<p>Whether a particular property is truly ancestral is often the central question in these disputes, and it depends on its history: how it was acquired, whether it was partitioned, and how it passed down.</p>
<h2>When can a grandson inherit?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Through his parent:</strong> when his father or mother inherits the property and it later passes to him.</li>
<li><strong>If his parent has died:</strong> he steps into his parent's place as a Class I heir.</li>
<li><strong>Under a will:</strong> if the grandfather leaves the property to him.</li>
<li><strong>In coparcenary property:</strong> where a genuine coparcenary exists.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>A grandson does not automatically get a share in his grandfather's self-acquired property just by being born. His rights usually come through his parent, through a will, or through a genuine coparcenary. Because so much depends on the property's history, documents such as old partition deeds, sale deeds and revenue records are key evidence in these cases.</p>
HTML);
